  "script": "Two bots accidentally priced a used biology textbook at twenty-three million dollars, and nobody noticed for days. In April 2011, a postdoc named Michael needed a textbook about flies. Normal price? About a hundred bucks. Instead, two rival Amazon sellers were using automated pricing bots. Seller A set a simple rule: always match Seller B, minus a fraction. Seller B set an even greedier rule: always charge twenty-seven percent more than Seller A. Wait, what happens when two bots only copy each other? Every night, Seller B raised the price. By morning, Seller A caught up. Then Seller B jumped twenty-seven percent higher again. You see, it's like two auction bidders who both leave automatic instructions: whatever the other guy bids, go higher, and then both walk out of the room. Within ten days, that dusty paperback hit twenty-three million, six hundred ninety-eight thousand dollars. And the best part? It still charged three ninety-nine for shipping.",
